1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
When building a machinery model,

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:06,600
we want our model to generalize as well as possible.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,135
Let's consider the following example.

4
00:00:09,135 --> 00:00:10,650
In the first case,

5
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:12,825
shown by the graph on the left,

6
00:00:12,825 --> 00:00:18,540
we fit a linear regression model to our dataset as shown by the blue line.

7
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:20,175
In the other case,

8
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:21,615
shown on the right,

9
00:00:21,615 --> 00:00:25,010
we use a polynomial regression model which is

10
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:27,845
a much more complex machine learning model

11
00:00:27,845 --> 00:00:31,610
and we observed that this model fits the data precisely.

12
00:00:31,610 --> 00:00:37,565
However, how do you think each model is going to perform on new data?.

13
00:00:37,565 --> 00:00:41,000
The linear regression model will better perform on

14
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,980
this new data because it's not fitting the original data as closely.

15
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:51,640
We will say that this model generalizes well to new data.

16
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:57,950
However, the polynomial regression model will not perform well on the new data.

17
00:00:57,950 --> 00:01:02,890
We'll use the term overfitting to describe this behavior.

18
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:06,470
The challenge of machine learning lies in creating

19
00:01:06,470 --> 00:01:11,675
a model that performs well on the available data without overfitting.

20
00:01:11,675 --> 00:01:15,985
We'll cover these in depth in later sections.

21
00:01:15,985 --> 00:01:21,710
From now on, we will use the term Training datasets to describe

22
00:01:21,710 --> 00:01:27,980
the data that our models have been trained on and the term Test dataset

23
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:31,475
to describe data that has not been used during training.

24
00:01:31,475 --> 00:01:35,260
We'll use the test dataset to solve this overfitting issue.

25
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,869
In the following videos,

26
00:01:36,869 --> 00:01:40,800
we'll describe how to create such datasets.

